1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,460
Nine hundred and ninety-nine pounds
and ninety-nine pence.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:04,820
Add one penny.

3
00:00:05,493 --> 00:00:07,113
The answer is zero.

4
00:00:08,272 --> 00:00:10,652
No error message. No crash.

5
00:00:10,732 --> 00:00:13,632
Just zero,
written quietly into the account.

6
00:00:14,301 --> 00:00:17,801
In COBOL, when the field for a result
is too small to hold it,

7
00:00:17,881 --> 00:00:20,481
the extra digit can simply disappear.

8
00:00:21,481 --> 00:00:25,441
Unless the program asks to be warned,
the calculation carries on.

9
00:00:26,026 --> 00:00:27,666
It doesn't round, either.

10
00:00:28,286 --> 00:00:30,146
Two pounds, thirty-four and a half pence,

11
00:00:30,376 --> 00:00:32,206
stored to the penny,
becomes two thirty-four.

12
00:00:33,441 --> 00:00:38,016
Write one extra word, ROUNDED,
and it becomes two thirty-five.

13
00:00:38,316 --> 00:00:39,436
Neither of these is a bug.

14
00:00:39,666 --> 00:00:41,416
They're rules.

15
00:00:41,556 --> 00:00:45,336
And many of the world's banks
still run on rules like these.

16
00:00:46,696 --> 00:00:49,896
COBOL is a programming language from 1959.

17
00:00:50,556 --> 00:00:53,436
Not every program written
in it is that old.

18
00:00:53,756 --> 00:00:56,496
But the language is,
and so are its habits.

19
00:00:57,313 --> 00:01:00,833
So why haven't banks replaced it?
They have the money.

20
00:01:01,133 --> 00:01:02,433
They have the engineers.

21
00:01:03,343 --> 00:01:05,923
To find out,
we'll look at three real cases.

22
00:01:06,963 --> 00:01:09,283
A bank that tried to leave its old system,

23
00:01:09,383 --> 00:01:13,203
one that managed it,
and a crisis where everyone blamed COBOL.

24
00:01:14,081 --> 00:01:16,401
But first, that missing half penny.

25
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:20,300
Say you rewrite that banking program
in a modern language.

26
00:01:20,420 --> 00:01:22,740
You'd probably fix those rules.

27
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:25,200
Overflow becomes an error.

28
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:27,880
Half a penny gets rounded up.

29
00:01:28,402 --> 00:01:30,242
Cleaner. Safer.

30
00:01:30,602 --> 00:01:35,102
And now, wherever half a penny turns up,
your program disagrees with the old one.

31
00:01:35,605 --> 00:01:38,685
Modern languages can do
exact decimal maths.

32
00:01:39,125 --> 00:01:41,465
Java has a type built for it.

33
00:01:41,865 --> 00:01:44,345
But even there,
the rounding rule is a choice.

34
00:01:45,070 --> 00:01:48,690
One common setting rounds the half up:
two thirty-five.

35
00:01:48,890 --> 00:01:54,250
Another rounds to the nearest even digit:
two thirty-four. Both are correct.

36
00:01:54,570 --> 00:01:55,930
Only one matches.

37
00:01:56,371 --> 00:01:58,031
And with ordinary floating point,

38
00:01:58,111 --> 00:02:01,771
the kind most code uses by default,
it gets stranger.

39
00:02:02,491 --> 00:02:06,891
Zero point one plus zero point two
isn't zero point three.

40
00:02:07,477 --> 00:02:09,837
So replacing COBOL isn't translation.

41
00:02:10,837 --> 00:02:14,057
It's finding every rule like this,
in millions of lines,

42
00:02:14,137 --> 00:02:16,457
and keeping each one exactly as it was.

43
00:02:19,107 --> 00:02:21,487
How did it end up there
in the first place?

44
00:02:22,411 --> 00:02:27,971
In May 1959, the US Department of Defense
called a meeting at the Pentagon.

45
00:02:29,311 --> 00:02:30,831
Computer makers, government agencies

46
00:02:31,061 --> 00:02:34,451
and big companies came,
all with the same problem.

47
00:02:35,074 --> 00:02:37,514
Every computer had its own language.

48
00:02:38,194 --> 00:02:41,694
A payroll program written for one machine
was useless on another.

49
00:02:42,255 --> 00:02:45,095
A committee designed a common language
for business.

50
00:02:45,955 --> 00:02:50,975
Its biggest influence was FLOW-MATIC, from
Grace Hopper's team at Remington Rand.

51
00:02:51,418 --> 00:02:55,318
And in December 1960,
essentially the same COBOL program

52
00:02:55,548 --> 00:02:58,318
ran on two computers
from rival manufacturers.

53
00:02:59,458 --> 00:03:01,038
That was the whole point.

54
00:03:01,692 --> 00:03:04,232
It was designed so people could follow it.

55
00:03:04,812 --> 00:03:06,922
ADD PAYMENT TO ACCOUNT-BALANCE.

56
00:03:07,292 --> 00:03:09,392
You can probably guess what that does.

57
00:03:09,727 --> 00:03:13,387
And it stored money the way people
write it: as decimal digits.

58
00:03:14,347 --> 00:03:17,067
The decimal point
doesn't even have to be stored.

59
00:03:17,767 --> 00:03:21,907
The field's description says where it
goes: the last two digits are pence.

60
00:03:22,563 --> 00:03:27,033
In 1964, IBM launched the System/360.

61
00:03:27,683 --> 00:03:31,003
Its design included instructions
for decimal arithmetic.

62
00:03:31,641 --> 00:03:36,221
And IBM has designed its mainframes
to stay compatible ever since.

63
00:03:37,181 --> 00:03:41,581
Many older programs can still run on
today's machines without being rewritten.

64
00:03:42,020 --> 00:03:45,200
So there was rarely a moment
that forced anyone to move.

65
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:50,140
Every year the code kept working,
and every year it collected more rules.

66
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:54,140
New products, new taxes, new regulations,
new exceptions.

67
00:03:56,730 --> 00:04:00,130
Nobody has counted how much COBOL
is still running worldwide.

68
00:04:01,230 --> 00:04:04,250
But IBM says forty-five
of the world's fifty biggest banks

69
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:06,150
still use its mainframes.

70
00:04:06,597 --> 00:04:08,397
And some owners have counted.

71
00:04:09,377 --> 00:04:12,657
In 2017,
America's Social Security Administration

72
00:04:12,887 --> 00:04:17,337
said its systems held
over sixty million lines of COBOL.

73
00:04:17,767 --> 00:04:20,807
In 2025, US government auditors

74
00:04:21,037 --> 00:04:24,587
listed the eleven federal systems
most in need of replacement.

75
00:04:25,437 --> 00:04:28,197
They were between twenty-three
and sixty years old,

76
00:04:28,277 --> 00:04:32,257
and cost about seven hundred and fifty
million dollars a year to run.

77
00:04:33,450 --> 00:04:37,890
So the code is old, it's everywhere,
and it's full of rules nobody wrote down.

78
00:04:38,390 --> 00:04:40,950
Surely the danger is in keeping it.

79
00:04:41,505 --> 00:04:43,825
Let's see what happens when a bank leaves.

80
00:04:46,365 --> 00:04:49,165
In 2018, a British bank called TSB

81
00:04:49,395 --> 00:04:52,645
had about five million customers,
and a problem.

82
00:04:53,245 --> 00:04:55,585
Its systems were rented
from Lloyds Banking Group,

83
00:04:55,815 --> 00:04:59,365
a competitor,
and the fees were set to rise sharply.

84
00:04:59,697 --> 00:05:03,277
So TSB decided to move everything
onto a new platform,

85
00:05:03,357 --> 00:05:06,357
built by its new owner,
the Spanish bank Sabadell.

86
00:05:07,173 --> 00:05:09,013
One weekend in April.

87
00:05:09,613 --> 00:05:12,353
On Sunday evening,
the new system was switched on.

88
00:05:13,346 --> 00:05:17,306
On Monday, there were about
nine hundred thousand attempts to log in.

89
00:05:17,746 --> 00:05:21,586
Only about one internet banking login
in ten succeeded.

90
00:05:22,255 --> 00:05:23,495
Payments failed.

91
00:05:24,135 --> 00:05:27,195
Some customers logged in
and saw other people's accounts.

92
00:05:28,027 --> 00:05:30,087
The worst lasted about a week.

93
00:05:30,767 --> 00:05:34,287
TSB didn't get back to business as usual
until December.

94
00:05:35,059 --> 00:05:39,979
The fallout cost around three hundred
and thirty million pounds in 2018 alone.

95
00:05:40,439 --> 00:05:44,619
Regulators later added
nearly forty-nine million in fines.

96
00:05:45,861 --> 00:05:47,681
And here's the strange part.

97
00:05:48,201 --> 00:05:50,461
The customer data arrived intact.

98
00:05:51,121 --> 00:05:53,261
Both official reports say so.

99
00:05:53,511 --> 00:05:56,151
What failed was the new
platform around it.

100
00:05:56,831 --> 00:05:59,431
Two data centres, set up differently.

101
00:06:00,111 --> 00:06:01,951
Performance tested on only one of them,

102
00:06:02,181 --> 00:06:05,031
with the targets lowered
after the tests didn't pass.

103
00:06:05,386 --> 00:06:07,026
And there was no going back.

104
00:06:07,626 --> 00:06:10,826
Once the data had moved,
returning to the old system was,

105
00:06:11,056 --> 00:06:14,126
in the regulator's words,
"essentially impossible".

106
00:06:15,599 --> 00:06:18,359
TSB wasn't escaping a broken system.

107
00:06:19,019 --> 00:06:22,619
It was leaving one that ran,
because of who owned it and what it cost.

108
00:06:23,459 --> 00:06:25,559
Leaving was the dangerous part.

109
00:06:28,119 --> 00:06:30,279
So is leaving always a disaster?

110
00:06:31,580 --> 00:06:36,580
In 2008, Australia's Commonwealth Bank
set out to replace core systems

111
00:06:36,810 --> 00:06:39,860
that had served it
for more than forty-five years.

112
00:06:40,465 --> 00:06:42,705
The plan: about five hundred and eighty
million

113
00:06:42,935 --> 00:06:45,285
Australian dollars, and four years.

114
00:06:45,959 --> 00:06:47,239
It took five.

115
00:06:47,759 --> 00:06:49,139
Press reports later put the total

116
00:06:49,369 --> 00:06:53,039
at roughly one point one billion,
as the project expanded.

117
00:06:53,479 --> 00:06:55,939
But it didn't move everything
in one weekend.

118
00:06:56,739 --> 00:06:59,499
Deposit accounts went first, in 2010.

119
00:07:00,419 --> 00:07:04,239
Business accounts followed in 2011,
and loans in 2012.

120
00:07:04,907 --> 00:07:07,127
Each step was something the bank
could watch,

121
00:07:07,207 --> 00:07:10,167
check and learn from
before it bet on the next one.

122
00:07:10,678 --> 00:07:14,678
At the end, the bank says,
it became the first major Australian bank

123
00:07:14,908 --> 00:07:17,698
with real-time banking, around the clock.

124
00:07:18,390 --> 00:07:20,870
So it can be done.
But look at what it took.

125
00:07:21,410 --> 00:07:24,010
Five years, a much bigger bill,
and patience.

126
00:07:25,224 --> 00:07:27,344
Modernisation isn't a purchase.

127
00:07:28,004 --> 00:07:30,364
It's a project, with risks of its own.

128
00:07:32,764 --> 00:07:35,984
Now for the crisis
where everyone blamed COBOL.

129
00:07:36,908 --> 00:07:38,328
March 2020.

130
00:07:39,428 --> 00:07:43,248
As the pandemic shut down New Jersey,
new unemployment claims jumped

131
00:07:43,478 --> 00:07:47,949
from under ten thousand in a week
to over a hundred and fifty-five thousand.

132
00:07:48,249 --> 00:07:50,969
Sixteen times as many, in seven days.

133
00:07:51,649 --> 00:07:53,989
The unemployment office couldn't keep up.

134
00:07:54,576 --> 00:07:57,336
In early April, the governor
asked for volunteers.

135
00:07:58,336 --> 00:08:01,436
Not only health workers,
but people who knew COBOL.

136
00:08:01,616 --> 00:08:05,356
The state's systems, he said,
were more than forty years old.

137
00:08:05,940 --> 00:08:07,800
The story spread fast.

138
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:10,940
Ancient code had collapsed
under the pandemic.

139
00:08:12,227 --> 00:08:15,907
But when the state auditor
looked into it later, it found no evidence

140
00:08:16,137 --> 00:08:19,247
that the delays had been caused
by the computer systems.

141
00:08:20,046 --> 00:08:23,266
Separately, reporters who looked
at the error messages people were seeing

142
00:08:23,496 --> 00:08:26,126
traced many of them
to the website in front,

143
00:08:26,266 --> 00:08:28,766
written in a much newer language, Java.

144
00:08:29,746 --> 00:08:31,926
The old code behind it kept running.

145
00:08:32,581 --> 00:08:35,201
So why did the state
need COBOL programmers?

146
00:08:36,021 --> 00:08:38,541
Because Congress was changing the rules.

147
00:08:38,761 --> 00:08:42,021
New benefits, new amounts,
new people who qualified,

148
00:08:42,241 --> 00:08:44,681
and every change had to go
into the code, fast.

149
00:08:45,417 --> 00:08:47,837
The problem wasn't that the code was old.

150
00:08:48,437 --> 00:08:50,597
It was that few people could change it,

151
00:08:50,677 --> 00:08:54,037
and that the phone lines and the staff
were overwhelmed as well.

152
00:08:56,867 --> 00:08:58,067
Three cases.

153
00:08:58,567 --> 00:09:02,447
A bank that left a system that still ran,
and whose migration failed.

154
00:09:02,827 --> 00:09:05,527
A bank that left carefully, and succeeded.

155
00:09:06,347 --> 00:09:08,187
And a crisis where the old
code was blamed,

156
00:09:08,417 --> 00:09:10,607
and nobody could show it was the cause.

157
00:09:13,047 --> 00:09:15,647
That doesn't mean old systems never fail.

158
00:09:16,407 --> 00:09:20,527
They do. They get expensive,
the people who understand them retire,

159
00:09:20,757 --> 00:09:23,087
and waiting has a cost of its own.

160
00:09:23,343 --> 00:09:26,503
But age is easy to see,
and the real risks aren't.

161
00:09:27,043 --> 00:09:28,863
Rules nobody wrote down.

162
00:09:29,023 --> 00:09:31,043
Knowledge in a few people's heads.

163
00:09:31,563 --> 00:09:33,323
And the moment you switch over.

164
00:09:34,498 --> 00:09:37,958
That's why many banks don't replace
the old core in one go.

165
00:09:38,398 --> 00:09:39,598
They wrap it.

166
00:09:39,878 --> 00:09:44,258
The app on your phone is new, but it
may be talking to something much older.

167
00:09:44,623 --> 00:09:46,663
And they replace it piece by piece.

168
00:09:47,703 --> 00:09:52,943
In 2004, the software writer Martin Fowler
named this after the strangler fig,

169
00:09:53,023 --> 00:09:57,843
a rainforest plant that grows around
a tree until it can stand on its own.

170
00:09:58,075 --> 00:10:01,535
The old system keeps working
while the new one grows around it,

171
00:10:01,615 --> 00:10:05,315
one job at a time,
until nothing depends on the old one.

172
00:10:07,025 --> 00:10:09,285
Which brings us back to that half penny.

173
00:10:09,925 --> 00:10:11,965
Somewhere, a program may still drop it,

174
00:10:12,045 --> 00:10:14,845
because someone decided it should,
decades ago.

175
00:10:15,345 --> 00:10:19,085
Replacing that program means knowing that,
and everything like it.

176
00:10:20,765 --> 00:10:23,645
The old system isn't necessarily
what fails.

177
00:10:24,325 --> 00:10:26,785
Sometimes, it's what replaces it.

178
00:10:27,884 --> 00:10:31,824
Because replacing the technology
isn't the same as removing the risk.

179
00:10:32,544 --> 00:10:36,484
Sometimes it just moves the risk
into a single weekend in April.

180
00:10:38,674 --> 00:10:40,534
So here's a question for you.

181
00:10:40,854 --> 00:10:45,254
If a system has worked for forty years,
when is the right time to replace it?

182
00:10:45,334 --> 00:10:48,414
And have you ever had
to replace one yourself?

183
00:10:48,894 --> 00:10:50,144
Let me know in the comments.
